# Descriptors
-- a way of reusing the same access logic in multiple attributes (used in ORM field types e.g. in sqlalchemy)
-- descriptor is a class that implements a protocol consisting of __get__, __set__ and __delete__ methods (or some of them)
-- distinguishing python feature
-- @property, @classmethod, @staticmethod decorators leverage descriptors

-- terminology: 
descriptor class
managed class -- here the descriptor class instances are declared as managed attribute
descriptor instance
managed instance
storage attribute -- holds value of a managed attribute in the managed instance
managed attribute -- public attribute of a managed classed handled by a descriptor instance, with values stored in storage attribute
-- essentially a Template Method design pattern

Overriding vs Nonoverriding descriptors
-- reading an attribute through an instance returns attr defined in the instance, if none defined there, class attribute retrieved
-- assing attr in an instance creates attr there without affecting the class
-- **overriding descriptor** (aka data or enforcing desc.) -- implemens __set__ method
-- **nonoverriding** (aka nondata or shadowable desc.) -- no __set__ method, basically all Methods

Tips:
-- keep it simple -- property builtin is ok for many cases
-- read-only descriptors require __set__ (don't want them to be shadowed in instances)
-- validation descriptors can work with __set__ only
-- caching can be done efficiently with __get__ only
...
